Why each part differs from the proposal:

1. NO `position: sticky`. `.hqp-tbl` is `overflow: hidden` (inbox.astro:168) and `.hqp-grid` is `overflow-x: auto` (working tree) — both make the element a scroll container, and a sticky element resolves against its NEAREST scroll-container ancestor. Neither container ever scrolls vertically (its content is exactly its own rows; the PAGE scrolls), so `top: 0` would pin the header to a scrollport whose scrollTop is permanently 0 — a no-op. To make it pin on page scroll you would have to remove overflow from every ancestor, which would also kill `.hqp-grid`'s horizontal scroll, the whole point of that container. And even then `top: 0` is wrong: `.ps-bar` is `position: sticky; top: 0; z-index: 5` with `background: rgba(10, 8, 20, 0.92)` (inbox.astro:102-105), so a `z-index: 1` header would slide underneath it. Not worth it — a 10-row table does not need a pinned header.

2. NO `#1b1728`. Measured: #1b1728 against the #1c1631 data rows is 1.01:1, and against the working tree's #201a37 even rows 1.05:1. That is the same non-difference the finding complains about, just spelled with a hex. In this palette adjacent dark fills top out around 1.2:1 (#292144/--hline = 1.15:1; #302751 = 1.27:1), which is why the separator has to be a bright hairline: `inset 0 -1px 0 rgba(244,238,255,0.28)` is the only thing at this luminance that the eye actually reads as "the list starts here".

3. Type goes UP to 0.7rem, not down to 0.66rem. The proposal's own rationale says it "keeps the header quiet without shrinking it further" and then shrinks it from 0.68rem to 0.66rem. Weight + the rule do the work.

4. Drop `.hqp-tn` from the selector list — `grep -rn "hqp-tn" src/ worker-pulse/` returns zero hits. The class does not exist.

5. Keep the `:hover, :nth-child(even)` guards in the selector. The proposal drops them and relies on source order beating `.hqp-trow:hover` / `.hqp-trow:nth-child(even)` (equal 0,2,0 specificity). That happens to hold today only because the head rule sits later in the file; it silently breaks if anyone moves the block.

