The proposal has two halves. Half one is not implementable; half two is workable but ships broken CSS as written.

WHY IT FAILS AS WRITTEN:
1. "Give every <text> a `vector-effect`-style fix" — there is no such thing. `vector-effect` ships only `non-scaling-stroke` in every browser; SVG 2's `non-scaling-size` has no engine support. Cannot be done.
2. The fix's four custom properties do not exist. `grep -rn -- "--num-face\|--num-s\b\|--ink-val\|--lab\b\|hqp-endval" src/` returns NOTHING. The real tokens are `--num-xl/--num-lg/--num-md` (inbox.astro:63) and `--hink`/`--hdim` (inbox.astro:21). As written, `.hqp-endval` would render unstyled: default body font, 14px, inherited colour.
3. An HTML label needs a dynamic `top`, not just `left`. The y is `Math.max(T + 12, y(last.v) - 10)` (hq-pulse.js:136) and follows the line. A percentage of the wrap is also off by `.hqp-chart`'s own `padding: 0.5rem 0.4rem 0.2rem` (inbox.astro:89).
4. Collision with the existing tip. `.hqp-tip` is absolutely positioned in the same wrap at `top: 6px` (inbox.astro:92). When the last value is at max, the end label clamps to y = T+12 = 28 user units ≈ 23px from the wrap top at 393px — inside the tip's band. The HTML label must hide while `.hqp-tip` is visible.

THE SMALLER FIX THAT ACTUALLY WORKS — keep the SVG, re-derive the size from the rendered width (the proposal's own alternative, done correctly). Author CSS beats a presentation attribute, so the existing `font-size` attributes need not even be removed.

