The proposed fix is UNSOUND. I implemented `const fs = Math.round(12 / (wrap.clientWidth / W))` verbatim in the bench and measured it:

- **It overruns the left gutter.** `L = 40` (hq-pulse.js:109) is the plot's left inset, sized for 12-unit type. At 393px the fix picks `fs = 21`, and a realistic 5-glyph gridline label — `nfmt` emits `12.5k` and `1,240` — measures 51.3 user units wide starting at `x: 6` (hq-pulse.js:121), so its right edge lands at 57.3 against a 40-unit gutter. The label prints 17 units over the gridline and the area-fill gradient. At 360px it is worse: `fs = 23`, right edge 60.6.
- **It measures the wrong box.** `wrap` is `.hqp-chart`, whose `clientWidth` includes its own 0.4rem side padding: 364px, not the svg's 351.44px. The fix therefore lands at 11.53 CSS px, not the claimed 12.
- **It regresses desktop.** At 1280px it computes `fs = 10`, rendering at 12.16px — smaller than the 14.6px the desk reads today. The claim says it "stays 12 CSS px on desktop too"; it does, but that is a shrink, not a hold.
- **It bakes in one width.** `paint()` (pulse-shell.js:239-254) only re-runs on a room or range change, so a portrait→landscape rotation leaves phone-sized units on a wide chart.
- **It divides by zero on a detached or hidden host.** `12 / (0 / 640)` is `Infinity`, and `Math.round(Infinity)` sets `font-size="Infinity"`. The inbox path is safe by luck (inbox.astro:1280 un-hides `#bmPanePulse` before :1281 calls `pulseLoad()`), but hq-pulse.js is also rendered outside that pane — the bench page and the analyst overlay, per the warning at inbox.astro:47-51 — with no guard.

The claim's alternative ("halve the viewBox width to 320") is closer but arbitrary: it still leaves a fixed unit-to-pixel ratio that is wrong at every width except one, and 320×258 is nearly square.

CORRECTED FIX — stop scaling the type at all: size the viewBox from the render so one user unit is one CSS pixel. Then `L = 40` keeps its meaning (a 5-glyph label at font-size 12 measures ~29px, which fits), the three text sizes are literally 12/13/12 px on every screen, and nothing else in the function changes — `x()`, `y()`, the gradient, and the hover math in `at()` all read W and H from the closure.

Replace hq-pulse.js:107-112 with:

```js
export function lineChart(host, pts, opts) {
  const o = opts || {};
  const wrap = div('hqp-chart', null, host);
  // ⚠️ A FIXED 640-UNIT VIEWBOX SHRINKS THE TYPE. On a 393px phone the desk
  // renders this svg at 351px — a scale of 0.55 — so 12-unit labels came out
  // at 6.6 CSS px and no number on the chart could be read. Size the viewBox
  // from the render instead: one user unit is one CSS pixel, so 12 stays 12
  // and the L=40 gutter still fits a five-glyph label at every width.
  const cs = getComputedStyle(wrap);
  const inner = wrap.getBoundingClientRect().width
    - parseFloat(cs.paddingLeft || 0) - parseFloat(cs.paddingRight || 0);
  const W = Math.round(Math.max(300, inner || 640));   // || 640 = detached/hidden host
  const H = Math.round(Math.min(300, Math.max(190, W * 0.42)));
  const L = 40, R = 12, T = 16, B = 28;
  const svg = mk('svg', { viewBox: `0 0 ${W} ${H}`, class: 'hqp-svg', role: 'img',
    'aria-label': o.label || 'trend' }, wrap);
```

Two things to know about it:
1. The chart gets slightly taller on a phone — 190px instead of the 141.7px it renders today (258 × 0.5491) — and stays ~300px on desktop, close to today's 314px. That is the price of legible axis labels in a fixed aspect; if Trym wants the old height, drop the clamp floor to 170.
2. It still bakes in the width at render time. If rotation matters, wrap the body of the function in a `mount()` and re-run it from a `ResizeObserver` on `wrap` that fires only when the width moves more than ~24px, clearing `wrap` in place rather than re-appending to `host` (a re-append would move the chart to the bottom of its section).

